    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d544">

                <pb facs="0634" n="376" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg376"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 341.115P51/46</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-04-27">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 27, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 7.55 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1998. My 1995 [1955] of 19th.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d544-f1"/> Foreign Office
                    informs me that British Government never had any intention of seizing the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Joseph W. Fordney</hi>, but that, as the result
                    inquiries, they have decided to detain her cargo under order in council of March
                    11, on ground that the ostensible consignee does not exist at the port to which
                    the bills of lading were made out.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
